== 7. Relapses

#strong[The seventh device that Satan has to keep souls in a doubting and questioning condition is,]

BY suggesting to the soul his frequent relapses into the same sin that he has formerly pursued with particular sorrow, grief, shame and tears, and has prayed, complained and resolved against. Satan says, “Your heart is not right with God. Surely your state is not good. You only flatter yourself if you think that God will ever eternally acknowledge and embrace such a one as you are, who complain against sin and yet relapse into the same sin, who confess your sin with tears and groans and yet again and again fall into the same sin, etc.”

I confess this is a very sad condition for a soul: after he has obtained mercy and pity from the Lord, after God has spoken peace and pardon to him, wiped the tears from his eyes and set him on his feet, to return to folly. Ah! how relapses lay men open to the greatest afflictions and the worst#footnote[A backslider may say (#emph[opera & impensa periit]), “All my pains and cost are lost.”] temptations! How they make the wound bleed afresh! How they darken and cloud former assurances and evidences for heaven! How they put a sword into the hand of conscience to cut and slash the soul! They raise such fears, terrors, horrors and doubts in the soul that the soul cannot be as frequent in duty as before, nor as fervent in duty as before, nor as confident in duty as before, nor as bold, familiar and delighting in God in duty as before, nor as constant in duty as before. They give Satan an advantage to triumph over Christ. They make the work of repentance more difficult. They make a man’s life a burden, and they make death very terrible to the soul, etc.

#strong[Now the remedies against this device of Satan are as follows.]

The first #emph[remedy] against this device of Satan is solemnly to consider that there are many scriptures that clearly show it is possible for the saints to fall into the same sins of which they have formerly repented. #footnote[Hos 14:4.] “I will heal their backsliding; I will freely love them, for My anger has turned away from them,” says the Lord by the prophet Hosea. So the prophet Jeremiah speaks: #footnote[Jer 3:12, 14.] “Go, proclaim these words toward the north, and say: ‘Return, O backsliding Israel,’ declares the #smallcaps[Lord]. ‘I will no longer look on you#super[+] with anger, for I am merciful,’ declares the #smallcaps[Lord].#footnote[The sin of backsliding is a soul-wounding sin. “I will heal their backsliding”: you read of no armor for the back, though you do for the breast.] ‘I will not be angry forever.’ … ‘Return, O backsliding children,’ declares the #smallcaps[Lord], ‘for I am your#super[+] master, and I will take you, one from a city and two from a family, and bring you to Zion.’” So the psalmist: “They turned back and were faithless like their fathers, twisted like a faulty bow.” And no wonder; for though their repentance is ever so sincere and sound, yet their graces are only weak, and their mortification imperfect in#footnote[When a soldier boasted too much of a great scar on his forehead, Augustus Caesar (in whose time Christ was born) asked him whether he had not got it when he looked back as he fled.] this life. Though by grace they are freed from the dominion of sin, from the condemning power of every sin and from the love of all sin, yet grace does not free them from the seed of any one sin. Therefore it is possible for a soul to fall again and again into the same sin. If the fire is not wholly put out, who would think it impossible that it should catch and burn again and again?

The second remedy against this device of Satan is seriously to consider that God has nowhere bound himself by any particular promise that souls converted and united to Christ will not fall again and again into the same sins after conversion. I cannot find anywhere in the whole Book of God where he#footnote[In some cases the saints have found God better than his word. He promised the children of Israel only the land of Canaan, but besides that he gave them two other kingdoms, which he never promised. And he promised Zechariah to give him back his speech at the birth of the child, but besides that he gave him the gift of prophecy.] has promised any such strength or power against this or that particular sin that the soul will be forever (in this life) put beyond the possibility of falling again and again into the same sins. And where God has no mouth to speak, I must have no heart to believe. God will graciously pardon to his people those sins that he will not effectually subdue in his people in this life. I would go far to speak with the soul that can show a promise that, when our sorrow and grief for this or that sin has been so great or so much, God will then keep us from ever falling into the same sin. The sight of such a promise would be like life from the dead to many a precious soul who desires nothing more than to keep close to Christ, and fears nothing more than backsliding from Christ.

The third remedy against this device of Satan is seriously to consider that the most renowned saints, now crowned, relapsed in the days of their life on earth into one and the#footnote[A sheep may often slip into a bog, as well as a swine.] same sin. Lot was twice overcome with wine; John twice worshiped the angel; Abraham often dissembled and laid his wife open to adultery to save his own life, which some heathens would not have done: “So when#footnote[Gen 20:13; ch. 12.] God had me journey from my father’s house, I said to her, ‘This is how you can show your loyalty to me: Wherever we go, say of me, “He is my brother.”’” David in his#footnote[Perhaps the prodigal sets before us a Christian who has relapsed; for he was a son before, and with his father, and then went away from him and spent everything; and yet he was not quite undone, but returned again.] wrath was resolved, if ever a man was, to be the death of Nabal and all his innocent family; and after this he fell into the foul murder of Uriah. Though Christ told his disciples that his kingdom was not of this world, yet again and again and again, three separate times, they would insist on being on horseback; they longed to be high, great and glorious in this world. Their pride and ambitious spirit set them (who were only like so many beggars) striving for preeminence and greatness in the world, when their Lord and master had told them three separate times of his sufferings in the world and of his going out of the world. Jehoshaphat,#footnote[2 Chr 18:1, 2, 3, 30, 31.] though a godly man, still #emph[allies himself by marriage with wicked Ahab;] and though he was saved by a miracle, yet soon after he falls into the same sin and #emph[makes an alliance with#footnote[ch. 20:35, 36, 37.] Ahaziah king of Israel,] who acted very wickedly. Samson is numbered by the Spirit of the Lord among the faithful worthies,#footnote[Heb 11:32.] and yet he often fell into one gross sin, as is evident in the book of Judges.#footnote[Judg 14:15; ch. 16.] Peter, you know, relapsed often, and so did Jonah. And this comes about so that they may see their own inability to stand, to resist or to overcome any temptation or corruption; so that they may be taken off all false confidences and rest wholly on God, and only on God, and always on God; and for the praise and honor of the power, wisdom, skill, mercy and#footnote[The prodigal saw his father’s compassion as all the greater in receiving him after he had run away from him.] goodness of the physician of our souls, who can heal, help and cure when the disease is most dangerous, when the soul has relapsed and grows worse and worse, when others say there is no help for him in his God, and when his own heart and hopes are dying.

The fourth remedy against this device of Satan is to consider that there are relapses into enormities, and there are relapses into infirmities. Now it is not usual for God to leave his people#footnote[Relapses into enormities are (#emph[peccata vulnerantia & devastantia]) wounding and wasting sins. Therefore the Lord is graciously pleased to put his everlasting arms under his chosen ones and hold them up from frequently falling into them.] to relapse frequently into enormities; for by his Spirit and grace, by his smiles and frowns, by his word and rod, he usually preserves his people from relapsing frequently into enormities. Yet he does leave his choicest ones to relapse frequently into infirmities (and of his grace he pardons them as a matter of course), such as idle words, passion, vain thoughts, etc. Though gracious souls strive against these, complain of these and weep over these, yet the Lord, to keep them humble, leaves them to relapse frequently into these. And these frequent relapses into infirmities will never be their bane, because they are their burden.

The fifth remedy against this device of Satan is to consider that there are#footnote[There is a great difference between a sheep that falls into the mire through weakness and a swine that delights to wallow in the mire; between a woman who is forced, though she struggles and cries out, and an alluring adulteress.] involuntary relapses, and there are voluntary relapses. Involuntary relapses are when the resolution and full bent of the heart is against sin, when the soul strives with all its might against sin, by sighs and groans, by prayers and tears, and yet out of weakness is forced to fall back into sin, because there is not spiritual strength enough to overcome. Now though involuntary relapses must humble us, yet they must never discourage or deject us; for God will freely and readily pardon those as a matter of course. Voluntary relapses are when the soul longs and loves #emph[to return to the fleshpots of Egypt,] when it is a pleasure and a pastime to a man to return to his old ways. Such voluntary relapses declare the man blinded, hardened and ripened for ruin, etc.

The sixth #emph[remedy] against this #emph[device] of Satan is to consider that there is no such power or infinite virtue in the#footnote[Christ rebuked his disciples for their unbelief and hardness of heart, though they had seen his glory, the glory of the only begotten Son of God, full of grace and truth. John 1:14.] greatest horror or sorrow the soul can be under for sin, nor in the sweetest or choicest revelations of God’s grace and love to the soul, as to fence and secure the soul forever from relapsing into the same sin. Grace is only a created habit, which may be overcome by the secret, subtle and strong workings of sin in our hearts. And the revelations that God makes of his love, beauty and glory to the soul do not always remain in their freshness and power on the heart; by degrees they fade and wear off, and then the soul may return to folly again. We see this in Peter. After he had a glorious testimony#footnote[Matt 16:15, 16, 17, 18, 19, 22, 23, 24.] from Christ’s own mouth of his blessedness and happiness, he labors to keep Christ from going up to Jerusalem to suffer, out of base, slavish fears that he and his fellows could not be safe if their master were brought to suffer. And again, after this, Christ took him up onto the mount and there#footnote[Matt 17:1, 2, 3.] showed him his beauty and glory, to strengthen him against the hour of temptation that was coming on him. And yet soon after he had the honor and happiness of seeing the glory of the Lord (which most of the disciples did not have), he basely and most shamefully denies the Lord of glory, thinking#footnote[Matt 26:69–75.] by that means to provide for his own safety. And yet again, after Christ had broken his heart with a look of love for his most unlovely dealings, and had told those who first learned of his resurrection to go and tell Peter that he was risen, I say, after all this, slavish fears prevail over him, and he basely dissembles and plays the Jew with the Jews and the Gentile with the Gentiles, to the leading astray of Barnabas,#footnote[Gal 2:11, 12, 13.] etc.

Yet by way of caution, know that it is very rare for God to leave his beloved ones to relapse frequently into one and the same gross sin; for the law of nature is up in arms against gross sins, as well as the law of grace, so that a gracious soul cannot, dares not, will not frequently return to gross folly. And God has made even his dearest ones smart dearly for their relapses, as may be seen in his dealings with Samson, Jehoshaphat and Peter. Ah Lord! what a hard heart that man has who can see you stripping and whipping your dearest ones for their relapses, and yet makes nothing of returning to folly, etc.
